          "id": "priority-interrupt",
          "title": "Priority Interrupt",
          "subtitle": "CA452 Unit 3 Concept 10",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Priority Interrupt: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Priority Interrupt\n\n### Academic Definition\n\n**Priority Interrupt** is an interrupt handling mechanism in which, when multiple I/O devices request service simultaneously, the CPU determines which interrupt should be serviced first based on a predefined priority order. This is called **priority interrupt handling**.\n\nIn a system with multiple I/O devices, it is common for more than one device to raise an interrupt request at the same time. Without a priority mechanism, the CPU would have no deterministic way to decide which request to honor. The priority interrupt mechanism resolves this contention by selecting the highest-priority requesting device.\n\n### Working Principle\n\nThe fundamental working principle of priority interrupt handling can be described as follows:\n\n1. Multiple I/O devices may request service **simultaneously**.\n2. The CPU must determine **which interrupt should be serviced first**.\n3. A **Priority Resolver** receives all incoming interrupt requests and selects the one with the highest priority.\n4. The selected interrupt is forwarded to the **CPU** for servicing.\n\nThe general structure is illustrated below:\n\n```text\nDevice 1 ──┐\n Device 2 ──┤\n Device 3 ──┼──► Priority Resolver ───► CPU\n Device 4 ──┘\n```\n\nThe priority ordering among devices is typically arranged as:\n\n```text\nPriority\n\nHighest\n  │\n  ├── Device 1\n  ├── Device 2\n  ├── Device 3\n  └── Device 4\n  │\nLowest\n```\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\nConsider a system in which four devices are connected, with Device 1 having the highest priority and Device 4 the lowest.\n\n**Example — Simultaneous Requests from Device 1 and Device 3:**\n\nIf **Device 1 and Device 3 request interrupts simultaneously**, the priority mechanism selects **Device 1** because it has the **higher priority**.\n\nThis demonstrates the core behavior of priority interrupt handling: when contention occurs, the higher-priority device wins, regardless of the order in which the requests physically arrive.\n\n### Daisy-Chain Priority\n\nA common **hardware method** for implementing priority interrupts is **daisy chaining**.\n\n```text\nCPU\n │\n │ Interrupt Acknowledge\n ▼\n┌──────────┐\n│ Device 1 │\n└────┬─────┘\n     │\n     ▼\n┌──────────┐\n│ Device 2 │\n└────┬─────┘\n     │\n     ▼\n┌──────────┐\n│ Device 3 │\n└──────────┘\n```\n\nIn the daisy-chain arrangement:\n\n- The CPU issues an **Interrupt Acknowledge** signal.\n- This signal propagates serially through the devices, starting from the device nearest the CPU.\n- **The device nearest the CPU receives the highest priority.**\n- Each subsequent device in the chain has progressively lower priority.\n\nThus, in the diagram above, Device 1 (nearest the CPU) has the highest priority, followed by Device 2, and then Device 3.\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Trigger condition** | Multiple I/O devices request service simultaneously |\n| **Decision maker** | Priority Resolver (hardware) |\n| **Selection rule** | Highest-priority requesting device is serviced first |\n| **Example outcome** | Device 1 selected over Device 3 when both request simultaneously (Device 1 higher priority) |\n| **Hardware implementation** | Daisy chaining |\n| **Daisy-chain priority order** | Device nearest the CPU has the highest priority |\n| **Signal used in daisy chain** | Interrupt Acknowledge |\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- **Assuming arrival order determines service order.** In priority interrupt handling, the priority of the device — not the order of arrival — determines which interrupt is serviced first.\n- **Confusing priority with physical proximity in all schemes.** Physical proximity determines priority specifically in the **daisy-chain** method, where the device nearest the CPU receives the highest priority. This is a property of that particular hardware method.\n- **Forgetting the role of the Priority Resolver.** The resolver is the component that receives the multiple device requests and forwards the selected one to the CPU.\n- **Overlooking simultaneity.** The need for priority interrupt handling arises specifically when multiple devices request service **simultaneously**.\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Define priority interrupt handling clearly: it is the mechanism by which the CPU determines **which interrupt should be serviced first** when multiple I/O devices request service simultaneously.\n- Be ready to draw the **Priority Resolver** block diagram showing multiple devices feeding into the resolver and the resolver feeding the CPU.\n- Memorize the priority ladder example: Device 1 (Highest) → Device 2 → Device 3 → Device 4 (Lowest).\n- Practice the classic scenario: **Device 1 and Device 3 request simultaneously → Device 1 is selected** because it has higher priority.\n- For the **daisy-chain** method, remember the key rule: **the device nearest the CPU receives the highest priority**, and the CPU issues an **Interrupt Acknowledge** signal that propagates down the chain.\n- Be able to reproduce the daisy-chain diagram showing the CPU, the Interrupt Acknowledge line, and the series of devices (Device 1 → Device 2 → Device 3).",
