          "notes": "## Intel 8085 Microprocessor\n\nThe 8085 is an 8-bit microprocessor with an 8-bit data bus and a 16-bit address bus.\n\n```text\n8085\n                  │\n       ┌──────────┼──────────┐\n       ▼          ▼          ▼\n   Registers     ALU    Control Unit\n       │          │          │\n       └──────────┼──────────┘\n                  │\n          ┌───────┴───────┐\n          ▼               ▼\n      Data Bus        Address Bus\n       8-bit             16-bit\n```\n\nImportant registers include:\n\n```text\nAccumulator (A)\nB Register\nC Register\nD Register\nE Register\nH Register\nL Register\nProgram Counter (PC)\nStack Pointer (SP)\nFlag Register\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nThe **Intel 8085** is an **8-bit microprocessor** characterized by an **8-bit data bus** and a **16-bit address bus**. As an 8-bit microprocessor, its fundamental unit of data processing is one byte (8 bits), and the 8-bit data bus determines the width of data that can be transferred in a single operation. The 16-bit address bus determines the range of memory locations that the processor can directly address.\n\nArchitecturally, the 8085 is organized around three principal functional blocks — the **Registers**, the **ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit)**, and the **Control Unit** — which are interconnected and interface to the outside world through the **8-bit Data Bus** and the **16-bit Address Bus**.\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe 8085 operates through the coordinated action of its three core blocks:\n\n1. **Registers** — store data and addresses used during processing. The register set includes the **Accumulator (A)**, general-purpose registers **B, C, D, E, H, L**, the **Program Counter (PC)**, the **Stack Pointer (SP)**, and the **Flag Register**.\n2. **ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit)** — performs arithmetic and logic operations on data, working in conjunction with the Accumulator and the Flag Register.\n3. **Control Unit** — directs the overall operation of the processor, coordinating the registers and ALU.\n\nData flows between these blocks and the external system via the **8-bit Data Bus**, while memory locations are referenced via the **16-bit Address Bus**. The 8-bit data bus and 16-bit address bus together define the fundamental data-transfer and addressing characteristics of the processor.\n\n```text\n        Registers ──┐\n                    ├──► (internal interconnection)\n        ALU ────────┤\n                    │\n        Control Unit┘\n                    │\n          ┌─────────┴─────────┐\n          ▼                   ▼\n      Data Bus            Address Bus\n       8-bit               16-bit\n```\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Register Set Enumeration\n\nThe complete set of important registers in the 8085 is:\n\n```text\nAccumulator (A)\nB Register\nC Register\nD Register\nE Register\nH Register\nL Register\nProgram Counter (PC)\nStack Pointer (SP)\nFlag Register\n```\n\n- **Accumulator (A)** — a register listed among the important registers of the 8085.\n- **B, C, D, E, H, L** — the general-purpose registers listed.\n- **Program Counter (PC)** — a register listed among the important registers.\n- **Stack Pointer (SP)** — a register listed among the important registers.\n- **Flag Register** — a register listed among the important registers.\n\n### Example 2: Bus Width Interpretation\n\n- The **data bus** is **8-bit**, meaning data transfers are 8 bits wide.\n- The **address bus** is **16-bit**, meaning addresses are 16 bits wide.\n\n```text\nData Bus   : 8-bit   → 8-bit data transfers\nAddress Bus: 16-bit  → 16-bit addresses\n```\n\n### Example 3: Block-Level Data Path\n\n```text\nRegisters ──┐\n            ├──► Data Bus (8-bit) ──► External\nALU ────────┤\n            └──► Address Bus (16-bit) ──► External\nControl Unit┘\n```\n\nThe diagram shows the Registers, ALU, and Control Unit feeding into the **8-bit Data Bus** and the **16-bit Address Bus**.\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe bus widths of the 8085 can be expressed directly:\n\n- **Data bus width** = **8 bits**\n- **Address bus width** = **16 bits**\n\nSince the address bus is 16 bits wide, the number of distinct addressable locations is:\n\n$$\n2^{16} = 65536 \\text{ locations}\n$$\n\nThe data bus width of 8 bits means each data transfer carries:\n\n$$\n2^{8} = 256 \\text{ distinct values}\n$$\n\nThese formulations follow directly from the stated bus widths: an 8-bit data bus and a 16-bit address bus.\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Feature | Intel 8085 (as given) |\n|---|---|\n| Type | 8-bit microprocessor |\n| Data Bus | 8-bit |\n| Address Bus | 16-bit |\n| Core Blocks | Registers, ALU, Control Unit |\n| Key Registers | Accumulator (A), B, C, D, E, H, L, PC, SP, Flag Register |\n\n| Bus | Width | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data Bus | 8-bit | Carries data |\n| Address Bus | 16-bit | Carries addresses |\n\n| Register | Listed in Notes |\n|---|---|\n| Accumulator (A) | Yes |\n| B Register | Yes |\n| C Register | Yes |\n| D Register | Yes |\n| E Register | Yes |\n| H Register | Yes |\n| L Register | Yes |\n| Program Counter (PC) | Yes |\n| Stack Pointer (SP) | Yes |\n| Flag Register | Yes |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing bus widths** — the 8085 has an **8-bit data bus** and a **16-bit address bus**; do not swap these.\n- **Misclassifying the processor** — the 8085 is an **8-bit microprocessor**, not a 16-bit or 32-bit processor.\n- **Omitting registers** — the important registers include the **Accumulator (A)**, **B, C, D, E, H, L**, **Program Counter (PC)**, **Stack Pointer (SP)**, and **Flag Register**; ensure none are left out.\n- **Overlooking the core blocks** — the three core blocks are **Registers**, **ALU**, and **Control Unit**; all three connect to the buses.\n- **Forgetting the bus connections** — both the **Data Bus (8-bit)** and the **Address Bus (16-bit)** are shown emerging from the combined Registers/ALU/Control Unit structure.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- State clearly that the **8085 is an 8-bit microprocessor** with an **8-bit data bus** and a **16-bit address bus**.\n- Be ready to **draw the block diagram**: Registers, ALU, and Control Unit feeding into the **8-bit Data Bus** and **16-bit Address Bus**.\n- Memorize the **register list**: **Accumulator (A)**, **B**, **C**, **D**, **E**, **H**, **L**, **Program Counter (PC)**, **Stack Pointer (SP)**, and **Flag Register**.\n- Remember the three core blocks: **Registers**, **ALU**, **Control Unit**.\n- Use the bus widths to derive addressable locations: **16-bit address bus → \\(2^{16}\\) locations**, and **8-bit data bus → \\(2^{8}\\) values per transfer**.\n- Keep terminology consistent: **8-bit data bus**, **16-bit address bus**, **8-bit microprocessor**.",
