          "summary": "Complete study notes on 8085 Flags: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## 8085 Flags\n\nThe 8085 contains five major condition flags.\n\n```text\n┌─────┬─────┬─────┬─────┬─────┐\n│  S  │  Z  │  AC │  P  │  CY │\n└─────┴─────┴─────┴─────┴─────┘\n```\n\n**Sign Flag (S)**\n\nIndicates the sign of the result.\n\n```text\nS = 1 → Negative\nS = 0 → Positive\n```\n\n**Zero Flag (Z)**\n\n```text\nZ = 1 → Result is zero\nZ = 0 → Result is non-zero\n```\n\n**Auxiliary Carry (AC)**\n\nIndicates carry from bit 3 to bit 4 during arithmetic operations.\n\n**Parity Flag (P)**\n\n```text\nP = 1 → Even number of 1s\nP = 0 → Odd number of 1s\n```\n\n**Carry Flag (CY)**\n\nIndicates carry out of the most significant bit during addition or borrow during subtraction.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nThe **8085 Flags** are individual bits within the **Flag Register (F)** of the Intel 8085 microprocessor that record the **status of the Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)** after an arithmetic or logical operation. The flag register is an **8-bit register**, of which **five bits are defined as condition flags** — **S, Z, AC, P, and CY** — while the remaining three bits are **undefined/unused**. These flags are used by **conditional branch instructions** to alter the sequence of program execution based on the outcome of the previous operation.\n\nThe five flags are:\n\n| Flag | Full Name | Purpose |\n|------|-----------|---------|\n| **S** | Sign Flag | Sign of the result |\n| **Z** | Zero Flag | Whether result is zero |\n| **AC** | Auxiliary Carry Flag | Carry from bit 3 to bit 4 |\n| **P** | Parity Flag | Parity of the result |\n| **CY** | Carry Flag | Carry out / borrow |\n\n## Working Principle\n\nAfter every ALU operation, the 8085 **automatically updates** the condition flags to reflect the properties of the result. The flags are then **tested by conditional jump, call, and return instructions** to make decisions.\n\n- **Sign Flag (S)** — Set to the **Most Significant Bit (MSB)** of the result. If the MSB is 1, the result is treated as negative; if 0, positive.\n- **Zero Flag (Z)** — Set when **all bits of the result are 0**.\n- **Auxiliary Carry (AC)** — Set when a carry is **generated from bit 3 into bit 4** during arithmetic. Used internally for **BCD arithmetic** (e.g., by the `DAA` instruction).\n- **Parity Flag (P)** — Set when the result contains an **even number of 1s**; reset for an odd number of 1s.\n- **Carry Flag (CY)** — Set when there is a **carry out of the MSB** during addition, or a **borrow** during subtraction.\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1 — Addition producing carry and zero\n\n```text\n  1111 1111   (FFH)\n+ 0000 0001   (01H)\n-----------\n1 0000 0000   → result 00H, CY = 1\n```\n\n- **Z = 1** (result is zero)\n- **CY = 1** (carry out of MSB)\n- **S = 0** (MSB of 8-bit result is 0)\n- **P = 1** (zero 1s → even)\n\n### Example 2 — Result with odd parity\n\n```text\n  0000 0011   (03H)  → three 1s? No: two 1s\n```\n\nFor a result of **0000 0111 (07H)**:\n\n- Number of 1s = **3 (odd)** → **P = 0**\n- **Z = 0**, **S = 0**, **CY = 0**\n\n### Example 3 — Auxiliary Carry during BCD addition\n\n```text\n  0000 1000   (08H)\n+ 0000 1001   (09H)\n-----------\n  0001 0001   (11H)\n```\n\n- A carry is generated **from bit 3 to bit 4** → **AC = 1**\n- This signals the `DAA` instruction to adjust the result for BCD.\n\n### Example 4 — Subtraction producing borrow\n\n```text\n  0000 0010   (02H)\n- 0000 0101   (05H)\n-----------\n  1111 1101   (FDH)  with borrow\n```\n\n- **CY = 1** (borrow occurred)\n- **S = 1** (MSB = 1)\n- **Z = 0**\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe flag values can be expressed logically for an 8-bit result $R = r_7 r_6 r_5 r_4 r_3 r_2 r_1 r_0$:\n\n$$\nS = r_7\n$$\n\n$$\nZ = 1 \\iff R = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nAC = 1 \\iff \\text{carry from bit 3 to bit 4}\n$$\n\n$$\nP = 1 \\iff \\sum_{i=0}^{7} r_i \\equiv 0 \\pmod{2}\n$$\n\n$$\nCY = 1 \\iff \\text{carry out of MSB (bit 7)}\n$$\n\nThese relationships show that each flag is a **Boolean function** of the result bits and the carry/borrow generated during the operation.\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Flag | Trigger Condition | Typical Use |\n|------|-------------------|-------------|\n| **S** | MSB of result = 1 | Signed comparisons |\n| **Z** | Result = 0 | Equality checks |\n| **AC** | Carry from bit 3 → bit 4 | BCD arithmetic (`DAA`) |\n| **P** | Even number of 1s | Data transmission error checks |\n| **CY** | Carry out / borrow | Multi-byte arithmetic, comparisons |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing AC with CY.** AC is the carry from **bit 3 to bit 4**; CY is the carry out of **bit 7 (MSB)**.\n- **Assuming S indicates true sign.** S simply reflects the **MSB**; it is only meaningful as a sign in signed arithmetic.\n- **Misreading parity.** P = 1 means an **even** number of 1s, not an odd number.\n- **Forgetting that flags are updated only by ALU operations.** Data transfer instructions generally do **not** affect the flags.\n- **Treating undefined flag bits as meaningful.** Only **five** bits are defined.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Memorize the **order and names** of the five flags: **S, Z, AC, P, CY**.\n- Remember the **mnemonic**: *\"Some Zebras Are Pretty Clever\"* for **S-Z-AC-P-CY**.\n- Practice computing flags for **addition and subtraction** examples, especially those producing **carry, borrow, and zero**.\n- Link **AC** specifically to **BCD arithmetic** and the **`DAA`** instruction.\n- Link **CY** to **multi-byte addition/subtraction** and **comparison** instructions.\n- Remember **P = 1 → even parity**, **P = 0 → odd parity** — a frequent trap in objective questions.\n- Always state that only **five of the eight bits** in the flag register are defined.",
