          "id": "simd",
          "title": "SIMD",
          "subtitle": "CA452 Unit 4 Concept 8",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on SIMD: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## SIMD\n\nSIMD means Single Instruction, Multiple Data.\n\nA single instruction is simultaneously applied to multiple data elements.\n\n```text\nInstruction\n                       │\n          ┌────────────┼────────────┐\n          │            │            │\n          ▼            ▼            ▼\n         PE1          PE2          PE3\n          │            │            │\n         D1           D2           D3\n          │            │            │\n          ▼            ▼            ▼\n         R1           R2           R3\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nA = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nB = [5, 6, 7, 8]\n\nA + B\n```\n\nThe same addition operation can be applied simultaneously:\n\n```text\n1 + 5 = 6\n2 + 6 = 8\n3 + 7 = 10\n4 + 8 = 12\n```\n\n```text\nADD instruction\n              │\n     ┌────────┼────────┐\n     ▼        ▼        ▼        ▼\n   1 + 5    2 + 6    3 + 7    4 + 8\n     │        │        │        │\n     ▼        ▼        ▼        ▼\n     6        8        10       12\n```\n\nSIMD is useful in image processing, graphics, scientific calculations and vector operations.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**SIMD (Single Instruction, Multiple Data)** is a class of parallel computing architecture in Flynn's Taxonomy in which **a single instruction** is issued by one control unit and **simultaneously applied to multiple data elements** across several processing elements (PEs).\n\n- **Single Instruction:** One operation (e.g., `ADD`) is fetched and decoded once.\n- **Multiple Data:** That same operation acts on many data operands in parallel.\n- Each **Processing Element (PE)** executes the same instruction on its own private data element.\n- Results are produced in parallel into corresponding result registers (R1, R2, R3, …).\n\nThis makes SIMD a form of **data-level parallelism (DLP)**, as opposed to instruction-level parallelism.\n\n## Working Principle\n\n1. **Fetch & Decode Once:** The control unit fetches and decodes a single instruction (e.g., `ADD`).\n2. **Broadcast:** The decoded instruction is broadcast to all processing elements (PE1, PE2, PE3, …).\n3. **Parallel Execution:** Each PE applies the same operation to its own data element (D1, D2, D3, …).\n4. **Parallel Write-back:** Each PE writes its result to its corresponding result location (R1, R2, R3, …).\n\n```text\n        ┌──────────────────────┐\n        │   Control Unit       │\n        │  (fetch/decode ADD)  │\n        └──────────┬───────────┘\n                   │ broadcast\n     ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐\n     ▼             ▼             ▼\n    PE1           PE2           PE3\n   D1            D2            D3\n     │             │             │\n     ▼             ▼             ▼\n    R1            R2            R3\n```\n\nBecause the instruction is shared, SIMD achieves **high throughput** with **low instruction overhead**.\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Vector Addition (from notes)\n\n```text\nA = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nB = [5, 6, 7, 8]\n\nA + B\n```\n\nThe same addition operation is applied simultaneously:\n\n```text\n1 + 5 = 6\n2 + 6 = 8\n3 + 7 = 10\n4 + 8 = 12\n```\n\n```text\nADD instruction\n              │\n     ┌────────┼────────┐\n     ▼        ▼        ▼        ▼\n   1 + 5    2 + 6    3 + 7    4 + 8\n     │        │        │        │\n     ▼        ▼        ▼        ▼\n     6        8        10       12\n```\n\nResult vector: `[6, 8, 10, 12]`.\n\n### Example 2: Image Processing\n\nIn image processing, each pixel can be treated as a data element. A single SIMD instruction (e.g., brightness adjustment) can be applied to many pixels at once, rather than looping pixel-by-pixel.\n\n### Example 3: Graphics\n\nIn graphics, vertex and color computations for many vertices can be performed with the same instruction applied across multiple data elements simultaneously.\n\n### Example 4: Scientific Calculations\n\nIn scientific calculations, large arrays of numeric values can be processed in parallel using the same operation, matching the vector operation pattern shown above.\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nSIMD vector addition can be expressed element-wise. For vectors:\n\n$$\nA = [a_1, a_2, a_3, a_4], \\quad B = [b_1, b_2, b_3, b_4]\n$$\n\nA single SIMD `ADD` instruction computes:\n\n$$\nC_i = a_i + b_i \\quad \\text{for } i = 1, 2, 3, 4\n$$\n\nFor the given example:\n\n$$\nA = [1, 2, 3, 4], \\quad B = [5, 6, 7, 8]\n$$\n\n$$\nC = [1+5,\\; 2+6,\\; 3+7,\\; 4+8] = [6, 8, 10, 12]\n$$\n\nGeneralizing to $n$ processing elements:\n\n$$\nC_i = A_i \\oplus B_i, \\quad i = 1, 2, \\dots, n\n$$\n\nwhere $\\oplus$ is the operation applied identically by every PE.\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | SIMD Behavior |\n|---|---|\n| Instruction stream | Single instruction issued once |\n| Data stream | Multiple data elements |\n| Processing elements | PE1, PE2, PE3, … operate in parallel |\n| Data elements | D1, D2, D3, … (one per PE) |\n| Results | R1, R2, R3, … (one per PE) |\n| Example operation | `ADD` applied to `[1,2,3,4]` and `[5,6,7,8]` |\n| Example results | `6, 8, 10, 12` |\n| Typical use cases | Image processing, graphics, scientific calculations, vector operations |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing SIMD with multiple instructions:** In SIMD, only **one** instruction is issued; it is applied to multiple data elements — not many different instructions.\n- **Assuming different operations per PE:** All PEs execute the **same** instruction; they differ only in the data they process.\n- **Mixing up data and results:** Each PE takes its own data element (D1, D2, D3) and produces its own result (R1, R2, R3).\n- **Forgetting the broadcast step:** The instruction is broadcast from the control unit to all PEs.\n- **Overlooking the element-wise nature:** The operation is applied element-by-element, e.g., `1+5`, `2+6`, `3+7`, `4+8`.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Always expand **SIMD** as **Single Instruction, Multiple Data**.\n- Draw the diagram showing one instruction branching to PE1, PE2, PE3 with data D1, D2, D3 and results R1, R2, R3.\n- Use the canonical example `A = [1, 2, 3, 4]`, `B = [5, 6, 7, 8]` giving `[6, 8, 10, 12]`.\n- State clearly that **one instruction** is applied **simultaneously** to multiple data elements.\n- Mention the key application areas: **image processing, graphics, scientific calculations, and vector operations**.\n- Emphasize the element-wise mapping: `1+5=6`, `2+6=8`, `3+7=10`, `4+8=12`.",
