          "id": "pipeline-speedup",
          "title": "Pipeline Speedup",
          "subtitle": "CA452 Unit 4 Concept 17",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Pipeline Speedup: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Pipeline Speedup\n\n### Academic Definition\n\n**Pipeline speedup** is a quantitative performance metric used in computer architecture to express how much faster a pipelined processor executes a program compared to an equivalent non-pipelined (sequential) processor. Formally, it is the ratio of the total execution time of a program on a non-pipelined machine to the total execution time of the same program on a pipelined machine, assuming identical clock cycle time and identical stage functionality.\n\nSpeedup is a dimensionless quantity. A value greater than 1 indicates that pipelining improves throughput; the theoretical upper bound is the number of pipeline stages, *k*.\n\n### Working Principle\n\nPipelining overlaps the execution of multiple instructions by partitioning instruction execution into *k* discrete stages, each handled by dedicated hardware. At any given clock cycle, up to *k* different instructions may be in flight, one per stage.\n\nThe key insight behind the speedup formula is:\n\n- **Non-pipelined execution** processes one instruction completely before starting the next. Each instruction consumes *k* cycles, so *n* instructions consume *n × k* cycles.\n- **Ideal pipelined execution** fills the pipeline once (taking *k* cycles for the first instruction to traverse all stages) and then completes one instruction per cycle thereafter. The remaining *n − 1* instructions each add one cycle, giving *k + n − 1* cycles total.\n\nThe ratio of these two quantities yields the speedup expression.\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\nSuppose:\n\n```text\nNumber of pipeline stages = k\nNumber of instructions = n\nEach stage takes one clock cycle.\n```\n\nWithout pipelining:\n\n```text\nTime = n × k cycles\n```\n\nWith ideal pipelining:\n\n```text\nTime = k + n - 1 cycles\n```\n\nTherefore:\n\n```text\nSpeedup =\n(n × k)\n────────────\n(k + n - 1)\n```\n\nFor a large number of instructions:\n\n```text\nSpeedup ≈ k\n```\n\nThus, a 5-stage ideal pipeline can theoretically approach a speedup of 5 for a sufficiently large instruction stream, although real processors experience stalls, hazards and other overheads.\n\n#### Worked Example 1: Small instruction stream\n\nLet *k* = 5 and *n* = 10.\n\n- Non-pipelined time = 10 × 5 = 50 cycles\n- Pipelined time = 5 + 10 − 1 = 14 cycles\n- Speedup = 50 / 14 ≈ **3.57**\n\nNotice that with only 10 instructions, the speedup is well below the ideal value of 5 because pipeline fill and drain overhead is significant relative to the total work.\n\n#### Worked Example 2: Large instruction stream\n\nLet *k* = 5 and *n* = 1000.\n\n- Non-pipelined time = 1000 × 5 = 5000 cycles\n- Pipelined time = 5 + 1000 − 1 = 1004 cycles\n- Speedup = 5000 / 1004 ≈ **4.98**\n\nHere the speedup approaches the theoretical maximum of *k* = 5, illustrating the asymptotic behaviour.\n\n#### Worked Example 3: Different pipeline depths\n\nLet *n* = 100.\n\n| k (stages) | Non-pipelined cycles | Pipelined cycles | Speedup |\n|-----------|---------------------|------------------|---------|\n| 3 | 300 | 102 | 2.94 |\n| 5 | 500 | 104 | 4.81 |\n| 8 | 800 | 107 | 7.48 |\n\nDeeper pipelines yield higher potential speedup, but only when the instruction stream is long enough to amortise fill/drain costs.\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe core speedup formula can be expressed in LaTeX as:\n\n$$\nS = \\frac{n \\cdot k}{k + n - 1}\n$$\n\nAs *n* → ∞:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} S = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{n \\cdot k}{k + n - 1} = k\n$$\n\nThis limit demonstrates that the maximum achievable speedup of an ideal *k*-stage pipeline is exactly *k*.\n\nAn alternative rearrangement highlights the fill/drain penalty:\n\n$$\nS = \\frac{k}{1 + \\frac{k - 1}{n}}\n$$\n\nThis form makes it explicit that as *n* grows, the term (k − 1)/n vanishes and *S* approaches *k*.\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Non-Pipelined | Ideal Pipelined |\n|--------|--------------|-----------------|\n| Execution model | One instruction at a time | Overlapped stages |\n| Total cycles for *n* instructions | n × k | k + n − 1 |\n| Throughput (instructions/cycle) | 1/k | ≈ 1 (steady state) |\n| Speedup | 1 (baseline) | Approaches *k* |\n| Fill/drain overhead | None | k cycles total |\n| Sensitivity to *n* | Linear | Asymptotic |\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Assuming speedup equals *k* for all *n*.** The ideal speedup of *k* is only approached when *n* is very large. For small *n*, fill/drain overhead reduces the effective speedup substantially.\n2. **Ignoring stalls and hazards.** The formula assumes an *ideal* pipeline. Real processors experience data hazards, control hazards, and structural hazards that insert bubbles, reducing actual speedup below the theoretical value.\n3. **Confusing cycle time with stage count.** The formula assumes each stage takes one clock cycle and that the clock cycle is the same for pipelined and non-pipelined versions. If pipelining increases the clock period (due to register overhead), the speedup is further reduced.\n4. **Forgetting that speedup is a ratio of times, not of cycle counts alone.** If clock periods differ between the two designs, cycle-count ratios do not directly give speedup.\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Always state the assumptions: *k* stages, *n* instructions, one cycle per stage, ideal pipeline with no stalls.\n- Memorise the two key formulas: non-pipelined time = *n × k*, pipelined time = *k + n − 1*.\n- When asked for speedup, show the substitution clearly and simplify.\n- If the question mentions hazards or stalls, note that the ideal formula gives an upper bound and actual speedup will be lower.\n- For \"large *n*\" questions, immediately state that speedup ≈ *k* and explain why (fill/drain cost becomes negligible).\n- Use the limit expression to justify the asymptotic result rigorously.",
