          "notes": "## Pipeline Hazards\n\nA pipeline hazard is a condition that prevents the next instruction from executing in its intended pipeline stage.\n\nMajor types are:\n\n```text\nPipeline Hazards\n                       │\n          ┌────────────┼────────────┐\n          ▼            ▼            ▼\n      Structural     Data        Control\n       Hazard       Hazard        Hazard\n```\n\n**Structural Hazard**\n\nOccurs when two operations require the same hardware resource simultaneously.\n\n```text\nI1 ─────► Memory ◄───── I2\n            ▲\n            │\n       Resource Conflict\n```\n\n---\n\n**Data Hazard**\n\nOccurs when one instruction depends on the result of an earlier instruction.\n\n```text\nI1: R1 ← R2 + R3\nI2: R4 ← R1 + R5\n             ▲\n             │\n       Depends on I1\n```\n\nThe second instruction needs the result produced by the first.\n\n---\n\n**Control Hazard**\n\nOccurs mainly due to branch or jump instructions.\n\n```text\nInstruction\n     │\n     ▼\n   Branch?\n   ┌─┴─┐\n  Yes  No\n   │    │\n   ▼    ▼\nNew    Next\nPath   Instruction\n```\n\nThe processor may not initially know which instruction should be fetched next.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nA **pipeline hazard** (also called a **pipeline stall** or **pipeline conflict**) is formally defined as any condition arising in an instruction pipeline that prevents the next instruction in the instruction stream from executing during its designated clock cycle. Hazards reduce the ideal speedup gained from pipelining and, if left unhandled, can produce incorrect results or degrade throughput.\n\nIn academic computer architecture literature, hazards are classified into three canonical categories:\n\n1. **Structural hazards** — resource conflicts.\n2. **Data hazards** — data dependency conflicts.\n3. **Control hazards** — branch/jump induced conflicts.\n\nThese categories are exhaustive with respect to the causes of pipeline disruption in a classic RISC-style pipeline.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\nA pipelined processor overlaps the execution of multiple instructions by dividing instruction execution into discrete stages (e.g., Fetch, Decode, Execute, Memory, Write-back). Ideally, one instruction completes every clock cycle. However, this ideal is violated when:\n\n- **Two instructions need the same hardware unit in the same cycle** → Structural Hazard.\n- **An instruction needs a value that a prior instruction has not yet produced** → Data Hazard.\n- **The next instruction address depends on an unresolved branch/jump** → Control Hazard.\n\nThe processor must detect the hazard and either **stall** the pipeline (insert bubbles), **forward/bypass** data, or **predict** the branch outcome to preserve correctness.\n\n```text\nIdeal Pipeline:\nCycle:    1    2    3    4    5\nI1:      IF   ID   EX   MEM  WB\nI2:           IF   ID   EX   MEM  WB\nI3:                IF   ID   EX   MEM  WB\n\nHazard causes a bubble:\nCycle:    1    2    3    4    5    6\nI1:      IF   ID   EX   MEM  WB\nI2:           IF   ID   STALL EX   MEM  WB\nI3:                IF   STALL ID   EX   MEM  WB\n```\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Structural Hazard Example\n\n```text\nI1: LOAD  R1, [A]     ; needs Memory in MEM stage\nI2: FETCH next instr  ; needs Memory in IF stage\n```\n\nBoth instructions require the **same memory unit** in the same cycle → structural hazard.\n\n### Data Hazard Example\n\n```text\nI1: R1 ← R2 + R3\nI2: R4 ← R1 + R5\n```\n\n`I2` reads `R1` before `I1` has written it back → data hazard.\n\n### Control Hazard Example\n\n```text\nI1: BEQ R1, R2, TARGET\nI2: ADD R3, R4, R5    ; fetched speculatively\n```\n\nIf the branch is taken, `I2` should not have been fetched → control hazard.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nLet:\n\n- \\( k \\) = number of pipeline stages\n- \\( n \\) = number of instructions\n- \\( T_{clk} \\) = clock cycle time\n- \\( S \\) = number of stall cycles introduced by hazards\n\n**Ideal pipeline execution time:**\n\n\\[\nT_{ideal} = (k + n - 1) \\cdot T_{clk}\n\\]\n\n**Actual pipeline execution time with stalls:**\n\n\\[\nT_{actual} = (k + n - 1 + S) \\cdot T_{clk}\n\\]\n\n**Pipeline speedup:**\n\n\\[\n\\text{Speedup} = \\frac{n \\cdot k \\cdot T_{clk}}{(k + n - 1 + S) \\cdot T_{clk}}\n\\]\n\n**CPI (Cycles Per Instruction) with hazards:**\n\n\\[\n\\text{CPI} = 1 + \\frac{S}{n}\n\\]\n\nAs \\( S \\to 0 \\), CPI approaches the ideal value of 1.\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Feature | Structural Hazard | Data Hazard | Control Hazard |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Cause** | Resource conflict | Data dependency | Branch/jump |\n| **Triggered by** | Two ops needing same hardware | Instruction needing prior result | Unresolved next PC |\n| **Example** | Memory used by IF and MEM | `R4 ← R1 + R5` after `R1 ← R2 + R3` | `BEQ` followed by speculative fetch |\n| **Typical Fix** | Duplicate hardware / stall | Forwarding / stall | Branch prediction / stall |\n| **Pipeline Effect** | Bubble insertion | Bubble or bypass | Wrong-path fetch |\n| **Detection Point** | Resource scheduler | Dependency checker | Branch unit |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Confusing data hazards with control hazards.** Data hazards involve register/memory value dependencies; control hazards involve the *program counter* and branch decisions.\n2. **Assuming forwarding eliminates all data hazards.** Forwarding cannot resolve load-use dependencies in a single cycle.\n3. **Believing structural hazards only occur in memory.** Any shared functional unit (ALU, register file port) can cause one.\n4. **Ignoring the cost of stalls.** Each stall cycle directly increases CPI and reduces speedup.\n5. **Treating hazards as errors.** Hazards are normal pipeline phenomena; they are *handled*, not *eliminated*.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Always **classify** the hazard first: Structural, Data, or Control.\n- Draw the **pipeline timing diagram** to justify your answer.\n- For data hazards, identify the **producer** and **consumer** instructions explicitly.\n- For control hazards, mention **branch prediction** or **delayed branching** as standard mitigations.\n- Use the **CPI formula** \\( \\text{CPI} = 1 + S/n \\) to quantify the impact of stalls.\n- Remember the **three-category taxonomy** — examiners frequently test whether you can distinguish them.\n- When asked for \"types of hazards,\" always present all three with a one-line definition and an example each.",
