          "id": "hypercube-interconnection",
          "title": "Hypercube Interconnection",
          "subtitle": "CA452 Unit 5 Concept 12",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Hypercube Interconnection: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Hypercube Interconnection\n\nA hypercube is an interconnection structure in which processing elements are connected according to the dimensions of a binary cube.\n\nA 3-dimensional hypercube can be represented as:\n\n```text\n000────────001\n            /  │         /│\n          010────────011  │\n           │   │        │ │\n           │  100───────│101\n           │ /          │/\n          110──────────111\n```\n\nEach node is connected to nodes differing in exactly one binary bit.\n\nFor a n-dimensional hypercube:\n\n```text\nNumber of nodes = 2ⁿ\n```\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\n1-D → 2 nodes\n2-D → 4 nodes\n3-D → 8 nodes\n4-D → 16 nodes\n```\n\nThe structure provides multiple communication paths between processing elements.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nA **hypercube interconnection** (also called an **n-cube** or **binary n-cube**) is a topology for connecting processing elements in a parallel or distributed system, in which each node is assigned a unique binary address and is connected directly to every other node whose address differs in **exactly one binary bit**. The topology is defined by the dimensions of a binary cube, and for an n-dimensional hypercube the total number of nodes is given by:\n\n$$N = 2^n$$\n\nEach node therefore has exactly **n neighbors** (one per dimension), and the structure provides **multiple communication paths** between processing elements, making it a regular, symmetric, and scalable interconnection network.\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe hypercube derives its behavior from the geometry of a binary cube:\n\n1. **Binary addressing** — Every processing element is labeled with a distinct n-bit binary address (e.g., `000`, `001`, `010`, … `111` for a 3-D cube).\n2. **Single-bit adjacency** — Two nodes are connected if and only if their binary addresses differ in exactly one bit position. This single-bit difference defines one *dimension* of the cube.\n3. **Dimension-based links** — In an n-dimensional hypercube, each node has n links, one along each dimension. Traversing a link flips exactly one bit of the address.\n4. **Recursive construction** — A higher-dimensional cube is formed by taking two copies of the lower-dimensional cube and connecting corresponding nodes. This is why the node count doubles with each added dimension: 1-D → 2 nodes, 2-D → 4 nodes, 3-D → 8 nodes, 4-D → 16 nodes.\n5. **Multiple paths** — Because nodes are connected along several dimensions, more than one route generally exists between any pair of processing elements, which supports the structure's multiple communication paths.\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### 1-Dimensional Hypercube (2 nodes)\n\n```text\n0 ──── 1\n```\n\nTwo nodes; each node has one neighbor. Addresses differ in exactly one bit.\n\n### 2-Dimensional Hypercube (4 nodes)\n\n```text\n00 ──── 01\n│        │\n│        │\n10 ──── 11\n```\n\nFour nodes; each node has two neighbors (one per dimension).\n\n### 3-Dimensional Hypercube (8 nodes)\n\n```text\n000────────001\n            /  │         /│\n          010────────011  │\n           │   │        │ │\n           │  100───────│101\n           │ /          │/\n          110──────────111\n```\n\nEight nodes; each node has three neighbors. For example, node `000` is connected to `001`, `010`, and `100` — each differing in exactly one bit.\n\n### 4-Dimensional Hypercube (16 nodes)\n\nSixteen nodes, formed by doubling the 3-D cube, with each node having four neighbors.\n\n### Node-count progression\n\n```text\n1-D → 2 nodes\n2-D → 4 nodes\n3-D → 8 nodes\n4-D → 16 nodes\n```\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\n| Quantity | Expression |\n|---|---|\n| Number of nodes | $$N = 2^n$$ |\n| Dimension | $$n = \\log_2 N$$ |\n| Neighbors per node (degree) | $$n$$ |\n| Address length | $$n$$ bits |\n| Bit difference between adjacent nodes | exactly 1 bit |\n\nThe defining adjacency rule can be stated as: nodes $$A$$ and $$B$$ are connected **iff** the Hamming distance between their binary addresses is exactly 1.\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Feature | 1-D | 2-D | 3-D | 4-D |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Nodes (2ⁿ) | 2 | 4 | 8 | 16 |\n| Neighbors per node | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |\n| Address bits | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |\n| Example addresses | 0, 1 | 00–11 | 000–111 | 0000–1111 |\n\nAs dimension increases, both the number of nodes and the number of connections per node grow, while the single-bit adjacency rule remains unchanged.\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing node count with dimension.** The number of nodes is $$2^n$$, not $$n$$. A 3-D hypercube has 8 nodes, not 3.\n- **Assuming any two nodes are directly connected.** Only nodes differing in **exactly one binary bit** are directly linked.\n- **Miscounting neighbors.** Each node has exactly n neighbors in an n-dimensional hypercube — not more, not fewer.\n- **Forgetting the recursive doubling.** Each added dimension doubles the node count (2 → 4 → 8 → 16).\n- **Ignoring multiple paths.** The structure provides multiple communication paths between processing elements; assuming a single unique route is incorrect.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Memorize the core formula: **Number of nodes = 2ⁿ**.\n- Remember the progression: **1-D → 2, 2-D → 4, 3-D → 8, 4-D → 16**.\n- State the adjacency rule precisely: nodes are connected when their addresses **differ in exactly one binary bit**.\n- Be ready to draw or interpret the 3-D cube diagram (`000` through `111`).\n- Link the definition back to the key property: the hypercube **provides multiple communication paths between processing elements**.\n- Use the relationship $$n = \\log_2 N$$ to move between node count and dimension.",
