          "id": "cache-coherence-problem",
          "title": "Cache Coherence Problem",
          "subtitle": "CA452 Unit 5 Concept 18",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Cache Coherence Problem: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Cache Coherence Problem\n\n### Academic Definition\n\nThe **Cache Coherence Problem** is the inconsistency that arises in a **shared-memory multiprocessor system** when multiple processors maintain private caches of the same memory location. When one processor modifies its cached copy of a shared variable, the copies held in the caches of other processors become **stale**, meaning they no longer reflect the most recent value written to shared memory. Formally, cache coherence is the property that any read of a memory location returns the most recent write to that location, regardless of which processor performed the write and which processor performs the read.\n\nThe problem can be illustrated as:\n\n```text\nShared Memory\n                   X=10\n                    │\n          ┌─────────┼─────────┐\n          ▼         ▼         ▼\n       Cache P1  Cache P2  Cache P3\n          │         │         │\n         10        10        10\n          │\n       P1 writes\n         X=20\n          │\n          ▼\n        Cache P1\n         X=20\n\nCache P2 → X=10  ← stale\nCache P3 → X=10  ← stale\n```\n\nA coherence protocol must ensure that stale copies are invalidated or updated appropriately.\n\n---\n\n### Working Principle\n\nThe scenario above unfolds through the following sequence of events:\n\n1. **Initial state:** Shared memory holds the value `X=10`. All three processors (P1, P2, P3) read `X` and each caches a local copy with the value `10`.\n2. **Write event:** Processor **P1 writes `X=20`**. This update is applied to P1's local cache.\n3. **Divergence:** Because P1's cache now holds `X=20` while P2's and P3's caches still hold `X=10`, the cached copies have **diverged**.\n4. **Stale copies:** The copies in **Cache P2** and **Cache P3** are now **stale** — they do not reflect the latest value of `X`.\n5. **Coherence requirement:** A **coherence protocol** must intervene so that the stale copies are either **invalidated** (removed/marked invalid) or **updated** (written with the new value) appropriately.\n\nWithout such a protocol, P2 or P3 could read `X` and observe the outdated value `10`, violating the coherence property that a read must return the most recent write.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\n**Example — The illustrated three-processor case:**\n\n- Shared memory: `X=10`\n- Cache P1, Cache P2, Cache P3 each hold `10`.\n- P1 writes `X=20` → Cache P1 holds `X=20`.\n- Cache P2 holds `X=10` → **stale**.\n- Cache P3 holds `X=10` → **stale**.\n\nHere, two of the three caches hold stale data after a single write by P1. The coherence protocol's job is to reconcile Cache P2 and Cache P3 with the new value.\n\n**Two possible reconciliation strategies (as implied by the notes):**\n\n| Strategy | Action on stale copies | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Invalidation** | Stale copies are marked invalid / removed | P2 and P3 must re-fetch `X` on next access |\n| **Update** | Stale copies are written with the new value | P2 and P3 immediately hold `X=20` |\n\nThe notes state that a coherence protocol must ensure stale copies are **\"invalidated or updated appropriately\"** — capturing both families of solutions.\n\n---\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nLet the shared variable be `X`. Define the sequence of operations on `X` as writes `W₁, W₂, …` and reads `R₁, R₂, …`.\n\n**Coherence condition (informal statement from the notes):**\n\nFor any read `R` of location `X`, the value returned must be the value of the **most recent write** to `X` preceding `R`.\n\nLet `value(cache_i, X)` denote the value of `X` held in processor `i`'s cache. After P1 writes `X=20`:\n\n$$\n\\text{value}(\\text{Cache P1}, X) = 20\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{value}(\\text{Cache P2}, X) = 10 \\quad \\text{(stale)}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{value}(\\text{Cache P3}, X) = 10 \\quad \\text{(stale)}\n$$\n\n**Coherence requirement:**\n\n$$\n\\forall i,\\ \\text{value}(\\text{Cache } i, X) \\in \\{ \\text{latest written value} \\} \\ \\text{or the copy is invalid}\n$$\n\nThat is, every cached copy of `X` must either equal the latest written value or be marked invalid, so that no processor can read a stale value.\n\n---\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Before P1's write | After P1's write (no protocol) | After P1's write (with protocol) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Shared Memory `X` | 10 | 10 (or 20, depending on write policy) | Consistent with latest write |\n| Cache P1 `X` | 10 | **20** | 20 |\n| Cache P2 `X` | 10 | **10 (stale)** | 20 or invalid |\n| Cache P3 `X` | 10 | **10 (stale)** | 20 or invalid |\n| Coherence status | Coherent | **Incoherent** | Coherent |\n\n| Concept | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **Stale copy** | A cached value that no longer matches the most recent write |\n| **Invalidation** | Marking a stale copy invalid so it must be re-fetched |\n| **Update** | Writing the new value into stale copies |\n| **Coherence protocol** | Mechanism ensuring stale copies are invalidated or updated |\n\n---\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- **Assuming caches stay automatically consistent.** As the diagram shows, after P1 writes `X=20`, Cache P2 and Cache P3 still hold `10` — they do **not** update on their own.\n- **Confusing \"stale\" with \"invalid.\"** A stale copy holds an *outdated but present* value (`10`); it is not automatically marked invalid. The protocol must do that.\n- **Believing a single write updates all caches.** Only Cache P1 reflects `X=20`; the other caches remain stale until the protocol acts.\n- **Forgetting that both invalidation and update are valid remedies.** The notes explicitly allow either approach (\"invalidated or updated appropriately\").\n\n---\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- **Reproduce the diagram.** Being able to draw the shared-memory → three-cache → P1-writes flow is a high-value answer component.\n- **State the core problem in one line:** multiple private caches of the same location diverge after a write, leaving stale copies.\n- **Name the two remedies:** *invalidation* and *update* — these are the two ways a coherence protocol handles stale copies.\n- **Highlight the stale caches explicitly:** after P1 writes `X=20`, mark **Cache P2 → X=10 (stale)** and **Cache P3 → X=10 (stale)**.\n- **Define coherence precisely:** a read must return the most recent write, regardless of which processor wrote or read.\n- **Use the exact terminology** from the notes: *stale copies*, *coherence protocol*, *invalidated*, *updated*.",
