          "notes": "## Parallel Processing in Multiprocessors\n\nA problem can be divided into independent tasks.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nProblem\n                 │\n      ┌──────────┼──────────┐\n      ▼          ▼          ▼\n    Task A     Task B     Task C\n      │          │          │\n      ▼          ▼          ▼\n     P1         P2         P3\n      │          │          │\n      └──────────┼──────────┘\n                 ▼\n            Final Result\n```\n\nIf tasks are independent, they can execute simultaneously.\n\nThis reduces the total elapsed time compared with strictly sequential execution.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**Parallel Processing in Multiprocessors** is a computing paradigm in which a single problem is decomposed into multiple **independent tasks** that are distributed across multiple processors (P1, P2, P3, …) and executed **simultaneously**. The results produced by the individual processors are then combined to form the **Final Result**.\n\nFormally, a multiprocessor system contains more than one processing unit that can operate concurrently. When a problem can be partitioned such that its sub-tasks do not depend on one another, those sub-tasks may be assigned to distinct processors and run at the same time. This concurrency is what distinguishes parallel processing from strictly sequential execution, where each task must wait for the previous one to finish.\n\nThe key precondition stated in the notes is **task independence**: tasks A, B, and C must be capable of executing without waiting on each other's outputs.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe working principle follows the structure shown in the diagram:\n\n1. **Problem decomposition** — The overall problem is split at the root node into sub-tasks (Task A, Task B, Task C).\n2. **Task assignment** — Each independent task is mapped onto a separate processor (Task A → P1, Task B → P2, Task C → P3).\n3. **Simultaneous execution** — Because the tasks are independent, all processors execute their assigned tasks at the same time.\n4. **Result collection** — The outputs of P1, P2, and P3 are gathered at the merge point.\n5. **Final Result** — The collected outputs are combined into the single Final Result.\n\nThe critical property is that **no task blocks another**. If tasks were dependent, the simultaneous execution shown in the diagram would not be valid, and the processors would have to wait on one another.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Three Independent Tasks (from the notes)\n\nUsing the exact structure given:\n\n```text\nProblem\n                 │\n      ┌──────────┼──────────┐\n      ▼          ▼          ▼\n    Task A     Task B     Task C\n      │          │          ▼\n      ▼          ▼          ▼\n     P1         P2         P3\n      │          │          │\n      └──────────┼──────────┘\n                 ▼\n            Final Result\n```\n\n- Task A runs on P1.\n- Task B runs on P2.\n- Task C runs on P3.\n- All three run **simultaneously**.\n- Their outputs merge into the **Final Result**.\n\n### Example 2: Sequential vs. Parallel Contrast\n\n- **Sequential execution:** Task A → Task B → Task C, one after another. Total elapsed time is the sum of all three task durations.\n- **Parallel execution:** Task A, Task B, and Task C run at the same time on P1, P2, and P3. Total elapsed time is reduced compared with the strictly sequential case.\n\nThis contrast is the central benefit stated in the notes: *\"This reduces the total elapsed time compared with strictly sequential execution.\"*\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nLet the execution times of the independent tasks be $t_A$, $t_B$, and $t_C$.\n\n**Sequential elapsed time:**\n\n$$\nT_{\\text{seq}} = t_A + t_B + t_C\n$$\n\n**Parallel elapsed time (ideal, fully independent tasks on separate processors):**\n\n$$\nT_{\\text{par}} = \\max(t_A, t_B, t_C)\n$$\n\n**Speedup:**\n\n$$\nS = \\frac{T_{\\text{seq}}}{T_{\\text{par}}} = \\frac{t_A + t_B + t_C}{\\max(t_A, t_B, t_C)}\n$$\n\nThese formulations express the reduction in total elapsed time that the notes describe: when tasks are independent and run simultaneously, the elapsed time is governed by the slowest task rather than the sum of all tasks.\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Sequential Execution | Parallel Processing in Multiprocessors |\n|---|---|---|\n| Task execution | Task A → Task B → Task C | Task A, Task B, Task C simultaneously |\n| Processor usage | Single processor at a time | P1, P2, P3 each handle a task |\n| Requirement | None beyond ordering | Tasks must be **independent** |\n| Total elapsed time | Sum of task times | Reduced compared with sequential |\n| Result formation | Produced step by step | Merged from all processors into Final Result |\n| Diagram structure | Linear chain | Branch → parallel processors → merge |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Assuming all problems can be parallelized.** The notes specify that tasks must be **independent**. If tasks depend on each other, they cannot execute simultaneously as shown.\n- **Confusing parallel processing with pipelining.** Parallel processing runs independent tasks at the same time on different processors; it is not the same as overlapping stages of a single dependent chain.\n- **Forgetting the merge step.** The diagram explicitly ends with a merge into the **Final Result**; ignoring result collection breaks the model.\n- **Claiming elapsed time is always divided by the number of processors.** The notes only state that elapsed time is *reduced*; the reduction depends on task independence and the number of tasks/processors.\n- **Mismatching tasks to processors.** In the diagram, Task A → P1, Task B → P2, Task C → P3; each task is mapped to its own processor.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- **Draw the diagram.** Reproduce the tree structure: Problem → Task A/B/C → P1/P2/P3 → Final Result. It directly demonstrates the concept.\n- **State the precondition clearly.** Emphasize that tasks must be **independent** for simultaneous execution.\n- **Use the key phrase:** *\"reduces the total elapsed time compared with strictly sequential execution.\"*\n- **Contrast with sequential execution** to show understanding of the benefit.\n- **Mention the merge/collection step** — the Final Result is formed by combining outputs from all processors.\n- **Keep terminology consistent:** Problem, Task A/B/C, P1/P2/P3, Final Result.",
