          "id": "multiprocessor-performance",
          "title": "Multiprocessor Performance",
          "subtitle": "CA452 Unit 5 Concept 24",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Multiprocessor Performance: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Multiprocessor Performance\n\nImportant performance measures include:\n\n**Speedup**\n\nSpeedup measures how much faster a parallel system executes a task compared with a sequential system.\n\n```text\nSpeedup = Sequential Execution Time\n          ─────────────────────────\n          Parallel Execution Time\n```\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nSequential Time = 100 seconds\nParallel Time   = 25 seconds\n\nSpeedup = 100 / 25\n        = 4\n```\n\n**Efficiency**\n\nEfficiency measures how effectively processors are utilized.\n\n```text\nEfficiency = Speedup\n             ────────\n             Number of Processors\n```\n\nFor 4 processors and speedup 4:\n\n```text\nEfficiency = 4 / 4\n           = 1\n           = 100%\n```\n\nIn real systems, efficiency is usually below 100% because of communication, synchronization, load imbalance and sequential portions of the program.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**Multiprocessor performance** refers to the quantitative evaluation of how effectively a system containing multiple processors executes a given task relative to a single-processor (sequential) system. It is characterized by two primary metrics:\n\n- **Speedup** — the ratio of sequential execution time to parallel execution time.\n- **Efficiency** — the ratio of speedup to the number of processors, expressing how well the available processors are utilized.\n\nThese measures allow architects and programmers to determine whether adding processors yields proportional gains, and to identify bottlenecks such as communication, synchronization, load imbalance, and sequential portions of the program.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe working principle of multiprocessor performance evaluation rests on comparing two execution scenarios:\n\n1. **Sequential execution** — a single processor performs the entire task in time $T_{seq}$.\n2. **Parallel execution** — $N$ processors cooperate to perform the same task in time $T_{par}$.\n\nThe performance gain is then quantified as:\n\n- **Speedup** $S = \\dfrac{T_{seq}}{T_{par}}$\n- **Efficiency** $E = \\dfrac{S}{N}$\n\nThe ideal case is $S = N$ and $E = 1$ (100%), meaning every processor contributes fully. In practice, overheads reduce both speedup and efficiency:\n\n- **Communication** — processors exchange data, consuming time.\n- **Synchronization** — processors wait for one another at barriers or locks.\n- **Load imbalance** — some processors finish earlier and sit idle.\n- **Sequential portions** — parts of the program cannot be parallelized.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Ideal Case\n\n```text\nSequential Time = 100 seconds\nParallel Time   = 25 seconds\nProcessors      = 4\n\nSpeedup    = 100 / 25 = 4\nEfficiency = 4 / 4    = 1 = 100%\n```\n\nThis is the ideal scenario: speedup equals the number of processors, and efficiency is 100%.\n\n### Example 2: Realistic Case (with overheads)\n\n```text\nSequential Time = 100 seconds\nParallel Time   = 40 seconds\nProcessors      = 4\n\nSpeedup    = 100 / 40 = 2.5\nEfficiency = 2.5 / 4  = 0.625 = 62.5%\n```\n\nHere, communication, synchronization, load imbalance, and sequential portions prevent the system from reaching ideal efficiency.\n\n### Example 3: Interpreting Efficiency\n\n- **Efficiency = 100%** → Perfect utilization (ideal).\n- **Efficiency < 100%** → Some processors are underutilized due to overheads.\n- **Efficiency decreasing as processors increase** → Overheads grow faster than useful work.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\n**Speedup:**\n\n$$\nS = \\frac{T_{seq}}{T_{par}}\n$$\n\n**Efficiency:**\n\n$$\nE = \\frac{S}{N} = \\frac{T_{seq}}{N \\cdot T_{par}}\n$$\n\nWhere:\n\n- $S$ = Speedup\n- $E$ = Efficiency\n- $T_{seq}$ = Sequential execution time\n- $T_{par}$ = Parallel execution time\n- $N$ = Number of processors\n\n**Relationship between Speedup and Efficiency:**\n\n$$\nS = E \\times N\n$$\n\n**Ideal bounds:**\n\n$$\nS \\leq N \\quad \\text{and} \\quad E \\leq 1\n$$\n\nIn real systems:\n\n$$\nE < 1 \\quad \\text{(usually)}\n$$\n\nbecause of communication, synchronization, load imbalance, and sequential portions of the program.\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Metric | Formula | Ideal Value | Real-World Value | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|-------------|------------------|---------|\n| **Speedup** | $T_{seq} / T_{par}$ | $N$ (number of processors) | Less than $N$ | How much faster the parallel system is |\n| **Efficiency** | $S / N$ | $1$ (100%) | Below 100% | How effectively processors are utilized |\n\n| Scenario | Speedup | Efficiency | Interpretation |\n|----------|---------|------------|----------------|\n| Ideal (4 proc, 100s → 25s) | 4 | 100% | Perfect utilization |\n| Realistic (4 proc, 100s → 40s) | 2.5 | 62.5% | Overheads reduce gains |\n\n| Factor Reducing Performance | Effect |\n|-----------------------------|--------|\n| Communication | Adds time to parallel execution |\n| Synchronization | Processors wait for each other |\n| Load imbalance | Some processors idle |\n| Sequential portions | Cannot be parallelized |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Assuming speedup always equals the number of processors.** In real systems, speedup is usually less than $N$ due to overheads.\n2. **Confusing speedup with efficiency.** Speedup is a ratio of times; efficiency is speedup divided by the number of processors.\n3. **Reporting efficiency above 100%.** This is impossible under the given definitions; efficiency is bounded by 1.\n4. **Ignoring sequential portions.** Even small sequential parts limit achievable speedup.\n5. **Forgetting overheads.** Communication, synchronization, and load imbalance all reduce efficiency.\n6. **Using the wrong denominator.** Efficiency uses the number of processors, not the speedup value itself.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- **Memorize both formulas:**\n  - $\\text{Speedup} = \\dfrac{\\text{Sequential Execution Time}}{\\text{Parallel Execution Time}}$\n  - $\\text{Efficiency} = \\dfrac{\\text{Speedup}}{\\text{Number of Processors}}$\n- **Always show units and steps** in numerical problems (e.g., 100/25 = 4).\n- **State the ideal values:** Speedup = $N$, Efficiency = 1 (100%).\n- **List the four real-world overheads** when asked why efficiency is below 100%:\n  1. Communication\n  2. Synchronization\n  3. Load imbalance\n  4. Sequential portions of the program\n- **Use the relationship** $S = E \\times N$ to cross-check answers.\n- **Watch for trick questions** where speedup is given and efficiency is asked (or vice versa) — rearrange the formula accordingly.\n- **Remember the bounds:** $S \\leq N$ and $E \\leq 1$ under the given definitions.",
